<p>← LOOKING BACK &nbsp;|&nbsp; LOOKING AHEAD →</p>
<p>You now have the full shape of Aquinas's ethics: a theological framework that inherits Aristotle's teleology, a fourfold structure of law, and a natural law theory that claims moral universality and underwrites the right to disobey unjust statutes. In Unit 3, Kant will reject almost every move in this chain. Where Aquinas grounds morality in human nature, Kant will ground it in pure reason alone. Where Aquinas thinks reflection on what we are yields what we ought to do, Kant will argue that this move illegitimately derives "ought" from "is." The contrast could not be sharper.</p>
